{"id": "Mercury_417147", "question": "The salt in ocean water comes from all of the following except", "step_idx": 2, "options": ["A): eroding land.", "B): melting glacial ice.", "C): volcanic emissions.", "D): reactions on the sea floor."], "correct": "B", "initial_cot": "1. Salt in ocean water comes from various sources.\n2. Eroding land (A) contributes to the salt content as rocks containing minerals dissolve and release salts into the water.\n3. Melting glacial ice (B) also adds salt to the ocean as glaciers contain trapped salts that are released when they melt.\n4. Volcanic emissions (C) can release salts into the atmosphere, which can then be deposited into the ocean through precipitation.\n5. Reactions on the sea floor (D) do not directly contribute to the salt content in ocean water.", "initial_cot_probs": [9.227306918546674e-09, 9.227306918546674e-09, 1.9534208917093565e-08, 2.4274148017866537e-05], "initial_probs": [1.0368129551352467e-05, 1.1748629731300753e-05, 4.1006751416716725e-05, 0.00014312761777546257], "prediction": 3, "cot_prediction": 3, "adapter_id": "Mercury_417147_step_2", "global_job_index": 65, "cot_step": "2.", "segmented_cot": ["1.", "Salt in ocean water comes from various sources.", "2.", "Eroding land (A) contributes to the salt content as rocks containing minerals dissolve and release salts into the water.", "3.", "Melting glacial ice (B) also adds salt to the ocean as glaciers contain trapped salts that are released when they melt.", "4.", "Volcanic emissions (C) can release salts into the atmosphere, which can then be deposited into the ocean through precipitation.", "5.", "Reactions on the sea floor (D) do not directly contribute to the salt content in ocean water."], "adapter_record": {"base_model_id": "microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct", "adapter_id": "Mercury_417147_step_2", "adapter_path": "../../../../adapters/arc-challenge/Phi-3/npo_KL_sentencize_s=True_lr=0.0001_rs=1001_pos=False_ff2=False_strong_2proc_s1/Mercury_417147_step_