{"id": "Mercury_7068513", "question": "Which geologic process most likely caused the formation of the Mount St. Helens Volcano?", "step_idx": 4, "options": ["A): converging boundaries", "B): diverging boundaries", "C): transform faults", "D): rift zones"], "correct": "A", "initial_cot": "1. Mount St. Helens is a volcano located in the Cascade Range of the Pacific Northwest region of the United States.\n2. The Cascade Range is part of the Pacific Ring of Fire, which is an area with a lot of volcanic and seismic activity.\n3. The Pacific Ring of Fire is associated with convergent plate boundaries, where two tectonic plates collide.\n4. In the case of Mount St. Helens, it is located on the boundary between the Juan de Fuca Plate and the North American Plate.\n5. The Juan de Fuca Plate is subducting (going under) the North American Plate, which is a process that occurs at convergent boundaries.\n6. The subduction of the Juan de Fuca Plate beneath the North American Plate leads to the formation of volcanoes in the Cascade Range, including Mount St. Helens.", "initial_cot_probs": [6.961379312997451e-06, 4.5984444030366944e-10, 1.317476266970985e-10, 1.5735010516770842e-11], "initial_probs": [0.00026067509315907955, 2.693549845389498e-07, 7.717149941299795e-08, 3.645323332079897e-08], "prediction": 0, "cot_prediction": 0, "adapter_id": "Mercury_7068513_step_4", "global_job_index": 507, "cot_step": "3.", "segmented_cot": ["1.", "Mount St. Helens is a volcano located in the Cascade Range of the Pacific Northwest region of the United States.", "2.", "The Cascade Range is part of the Pacific Ring of Fire, which is an area with a lot of volcanic and seismic activity.", "3.", "The Pacific Ring of Fire is associated with convergent plate boundaries, where two tectonic plates collide.", "4.", "In the case of Mount St. Helens, it is located on the boundary between the Juan de Fuca Plate and the North American Plate.", "5.", "The Juan de Fuca Plate is subducting (going under) the North American Plate, which is a process that occurs at convergent boundaries.", "6.", "The subduction of the Juan de Fuca Plate beneath the North American Plate leads to the formation of volcanoes in the Cascade Range, including Mount St. Helens."], "adapter_record": {"base_model_id": "microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct", "adapter_id": "Mercury_7068513_step_4", "adapter_path": "../../../../adapters/arc-challenge/Phi-3/npo_KL_sentencize_s=True_lr=0.0001_rs=1001_pos=False_ff2=False_strong_2proc_s1/Mercury_7068513_step_4.pt", "metadata": {"base_model_id": "microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct", "dataset": "arc-challenge", "strategy": "sentencize", "stepwise": true, "method": "npo_KL", "instance_id": "Mercury_7068513", "step_idx": 4, "target_index": 65, "global_job_index": 507, "question": "Which geologic process most likely caused the formation of the Mount St. Helens Volcano?"}}, "adapter_record_path": "v2_outputs/adapter_records/arc-challenge/Phi-3/npo_KL_sentencize_s=True_lr=0.0001_rs=1001_pos=False_ff2=False_strong_2proc_s1/Mercury_7068513_step_4.json", "adapter_training_history": [{"epoch": 1, "mean_loss": 13.862943649291992, "evaluation": null}, {"epoch": 2, "mean_loss": 11.284159660339355, "evaluation": null}, {"epoch": 3, "mean_loss": 7.522405624389648, "evaluation": null}, {"epoch": 4, "mean_loss": 4.538566589355469, "evaluation": null}, {"epoch": 5, "mean_loss": 2.841893196105957, "evaluation": {"completion": "A): converging boundaries.\n\nMount St", "probs": [0.00015817032544873655, 1.8519843081321596e-07, 3.646772839260848e-08, 1.7226136961312477e-08], "prediction": 0, "target_cot_step": "3.", "target_cot_step_prefix": "Human: Question: Which geologic process most likely caused the formation of the Mount St. Helens Volcano?\n\nChoices:\n(A): converging boundaries\n(B): diverging boundaries\n(C): transform faults\n(D): rift zones\n\nAssistant: Let's think step by step:\n\n1.\nMount St. Helens is a volcano located in the Cascade Range of the Pacific Northwest region