{"id": "Mercury_7234308", "question": "A scientist maps a long region in which earthquakes originate and determines this region is a transform plate boundary. Which evidence would cause the scientist to reevaluate this determination?", "step_idx": 1, "options": ["A): Volcanism also characterizes the region.", "B): Earthquake centers in the region occur at shallow depths.", "C): The region shows extensive faulting of sediments.", "D): Equal crust densities are found on opposite sides of the region."], "correct": "A", "initial_cot": "A transform plate boundary is a type of plate boundary where two plates slide past each other horizontally. This movement can cause earthquakes, but it is not typically associated with volcanism. Volcanism is more common at convergent and divergent plate boundaries, where plates collide or move apart, respectively.", "initial_cot_probs": [9.686126759334002e-06, 8.030079698073678e-06, 1.2314527566559264e-06, 1.48541550970549e-06], "initial_probs": [0.00022243089915718883, 0.00017322936037089676, 3.411088255234063e-05, 3.8652691728202626e-05], "prediction": 0, "cot_prediction": 0, "adapter_id": "Mercury_7234308_step_1", "global_job_index": 1381, "cot_step": "This movement can cause earthquakes, but it is not typically associated with volcanism.", "segmented_cot": ["A transform plate boundary is a type of plate boundary where two plates slide past each other horizontally.", "This movement can cause earthquakes, but it is not typically associated with volcanism.", "Volcanism is more common at convergent and divergent plate boundaries, where plates collide or move apart, respectively."], "adapter_record": {"base_model_id": "microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct", "adapter_id": "Mercury_7234308_step_1", "adapter_path": "../../../../adapters/arc-challenge/Phi-3/npo_KL_sentencize_s=True_lr=0.0001_rs=1001_pos=False_ff2=False_strong_2proc_s1/Mercury_7234308_step_1.pt", "metadata": {"base_model_id": "microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct", "dataset": "arc-cha